  "sampleHTML": [
    {
      "selector": "[class*=\"message\"]",
      "count": 27,
      "outerHTML": "<div class=\"chat-messages\"><div id=\"chat-message-container\" class=\"chat-container chat-container-bottom\"><div class=\"qwen-chat-message qwen-chat-message-user\"><div class=\"chat-user-message-container-wrapper ant-flex css-mncuj7\" style=\"gap: 10px;\"><div class=\"chat-user-message-container\"><div class=\"\" style=\"position: relative; width: 100%;\"><div class=\"chat-user-message-wrapper ant-flex css-mncuj7 ant-flex-justify-flex-end\"><div class=\"chat-user-message\"><div class=\"chunked-text-renderer-root chunked-text-renderer-root-with-expand is-collapsed\"><p class=\"whitespace-pre-wrap user-message-content fade-in \" style=\"line-height: 28px;\">hey see the previous chats re;ated to this topic and tell me , why did we add an expansion fee\r\n\r\nWe added the expansion fee because cloud infrastructure does not scale perfectly smoothly; it scales in \"steps\" or \"chunks.\"\r\n\r\nWhen you rightly pointed out that adding more clients (scaling toward that 882 target) increases the overall infrastructure burden, it highlighted a major financial risk. If the model simply divided the baseline cloud costs by the number of clients, you would eventually hit a wall.\r\n\r\nHere is exactly what the Expansion Fee protects your business against:\r\n\r\nThe \"Step-Function\" Reality: You cannot buy 5% of a new ETL pipeline or Data Integration Hub. Once your current shared infrastructure hits its capacity limit (e.g., at client #50), you must provision a whole new, expensive compute node. The expansion fee acts as a pre-funded financial buffer so the capital is already there to pay for that next \"step\" up in server capacity.\r\n\r\nUnpredictable Data Volume Explosions: In the pricing script, the Monthly Increment - Expansion Fee is tied directly to data growth pools. If a practice rapidly doubles its patient data volume, it requires exponentially more database indexing space and real-time compute power. This fee ensures their monthly bill automatically scales to cover the extra load they are putting on the FHIR servers.\r"
    },
    {
